    directAnswer:
      'Jayapura, the provincial capital of Papua in easternmost Indonesia, alongside the adjacent freshwater Lake Sentani basin and the rugged Cyclops Mountain Nature Reserve, constitutes one of the most prolific and distinctive agarwood terroirs in the Pacific-Melanesian biogeographical zone. Unlike western Indonesian islands (Sumatra and Kalimantan) where Aquilaria malaccensis dominates, the Jayapura terroir is characterized by botanical diversity centered on Aquilaria filaria and Gyrinops ledermannii. These species thrive in extreme humid tropical environments: coastal sago swamps, hyper-saturated peat lagoons, and pristine primary rainforests rising abruptly into cloud-forested mountain peaks. The resulting agarwood displays an unmistakable sensory architecture: intensely balsamic, medicinal, herbaceous, and sweet with undertones of green jungle ferns, crushed eucalyptus leaves, warm vanilla bean, and ocean ozone. Harvesters recover remarkable black structural cuts—frequently categorized as King Super and Double Super Papuan cuts—that possess sinking density and an intense, cool-boiling bubbling action on burning coals. In regional wholesale trade, Jayapura sinking wood commands between 2,200 and 7,500 USD per kilogram.',
